<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-dot-product-matrix-vector" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Dot Product and the Matrix-Vector Product</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now that we know what matrices and vectors are, we need to learn how to multiply them together.
    First we need to understand the <em>dot product</em> between two vectors.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: DOT PRODUCT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-dot-product">
    <title>Dot Product</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>dot product</term> between vectors <m>(a_1, \ldots, a_p)</m> and
        <m>(b_1, \ldots, b_p)</m> is equal to:
        <me>(a_1, \ldots, a_p) \cdot (b_1, \ldots, b_p) = a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2 + \cdots + a_p b_p.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DOT PRODUCT EXAMPLE (PROSE)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    For example,
    <me>(1, 2, 3) \cdot (-1, 0, 4) = 1 \cdot (-1) + 2 \cdot 0 + 3 \cdot 4 = -1 + 0 + 12 = 11.</me>
    Notice that the dot product gives a <em>scalar</em>, not another vector! We multiply the
    matching components together, then add everything up.
  </p>
<p>
    We can also write the dot product with column vectors. The process is exactly the same,
    and the components are even allowed to be functions:
    <me>\begin{bmatrix} e^t \\ 4 \end{bmatrix} \cdot \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ t \end{bmatrix}
      = 2e^t + 4t.</me>
  </p>
  <p>
    Also note that the two vectors must have the <em>same number of components</em> for the dot
    product to make sense. For instance,
    <me>(1, 3, 12, 0) \cdot (0, 0, 5)</me>
    is <alert>not defined</alert>, since the first vector has <m>4</m> components but the second
    has only <m>3</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: DOT PRODUCT PRACTICE (MC)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-dot-product-practice">
    <title>Dot Product Practice</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the dot product
        <me>(e^t, 2, -1) \cdot (3, t, 4).</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>3e^t + 2t - 4</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! Multiply the matching components and add:
            <m>e^t \cdot 3 + 2 \cdot t + (-1) \cdot 4 = 3e^t + 2t - 4</m>.
            The components involve functions, but the process is exactly the same.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(3e^t, 2t, -4)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close, but not quite! Multiplying the matching components is the right first step,
            but the dot product doesn't stop there: we then <em>add</em> everything up.
            The dot product gives a scalar, not a vector.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>3e^t + 2t + 4</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Watch the signs! The third pair of components is <m>(-1) \cdot 4 = -4</m>,
            so the last term should be subtracted.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Not defined.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Both vectors have <m>3</m> components, so the dot product is defined here.
            It's fine for the components to be functions like <m>e^t</m>: just multiply the
            matching components and add them up.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MATRIX-VECTOR MULTIPLICATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    With the dot product in hand, we can now multiply a matrix by a vector.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-matrix-vector-mult">
    <title>Matrix-Vector Multiplication</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and <m>\vec{x}</m> has <m>n</m>
        components. Then <m>A\vec{x}</m> is a vector with <m>m</m> components:
        <me>A\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} \vec{a}_1 \\ \vec{a}_2 \\ \vdots \\ \vec{a}_m \end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ \vdots \\ x_n \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} \vec{a}_1 \cdot \vec{x} \\ \vec{a}_2 \cdot \vec{x} \\ \vdots \\ \vec{a}_m \cdot \vec{x} \end{bmatrix},</me>
        where <m>\vec{a}_i</m> is the <m>i</m>th <em>row</em> of <m>A</m> (a row vector).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    In other words: to compute <m>A\vec{x}</m>, we use the <alert>rows of <m>A</m></alert> and the
    <alert>column <m>\vec{x}</m></alert>, taking a dot product for each entry. For example,
    <me>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
      = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 2 + 2 \cdot 3 \\ 3 \cdot 2 + 4 \cdot 3 \end{bmatrix}
      = \begin{bmatrix} 8 \\ 18 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
    Here a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix times a <m>2 \times 1</m> vector gave a <m>2 \times 1</m> vector.
    Notice how the sizes work: the number of <em>columns</em> of <m>A</m> must match the number of
    components of <m>\vec{x}</m>, and the result has as many components as <m>A</m> has <em>rows</em>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: Ax WITH FUNCTION ENTRIES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-matrix-vector-Ax">
    <title>Computing <m>A\mathbf{x}</m></title>
    <p>
      Let
      <me>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}, \quad
        \mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} e^t \\ \sin(t) \end{bmatrix}.</me>
      Determine if the product <m>A\mathbf{x}</m> is defined. If so, compute it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here <m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 2</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is <m>2 \times 1</m>.
      The number of columns of <m>A</m> matches the number of components of <m>\mathbf{x}</m>
      (both are <m>2</m>), so the product <em>is</em> defined, and the result will be
      <m>3 \times 1</m>. Taking the dot product of each row of <m>A</m> with <m>\mathbf{x}</m>:
      <me>A\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix} e^t \\ \sin(t) \end{bmatrix}
        = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot e^t + 2\sin(t) \\ 3e^t - \sin(t) \\ 2e^t + 0 \cdot \sin(t) \end{bmatrix}
        = \begin{bmatrix} e^t + 2\sin(t) \\ 3e^t - \sin(t) \\ 2e^t \end{bmatrix}.</me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: Ay UNDEFINED (MC)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-matrix-vector-Ay">
    <title>Is <m>A\mathbf{y}</m> Defined?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Keep the same matrix <m>A</m> from the example above, and let
        <me>\mathbf{y} = \begin{bmatrix} 5\cos(t) \\ \tan(t) \\ e^t \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        What happens when you compute <m>A\mathbf{y}</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>A\mathbf{y}</m> is not defined.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! <m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 2</m>, so its rows have only <m>2</m> components,
            but <m>\mathbf{y}</m> has <m>3</m>. The columns of <m>A</m> don't match up with the
            rows of <m>\mathbf{y}</m>, so we can't take the dot products: the product is undefined.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>A\mathbf{y} = \begin{bmatrix} 5\cos(t) + 2\tan(t) \\ 15\cos(t) - \tan(t) \\ 10\cos(t) \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This comes from dotting each row of <m>A</m> with just the first two components of
            <m>\mathbf{y}</m>, but that leaves <m>e^t</m> with nothing to pair with!
            Since the rows of <m>A</m> have <m>2</m> components and <m>\mathbf{y}</m> has
            <m>3</m>, the dot products (and the product <m>A\mathbf{y}</m>) are not defined.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>A\mathbf{y}</m> is defined, and the result is a <m>2 \times 1</m> vector.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Check the sizes again: <m>A</m> is <m>3 \times 2</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}</m> is
            <m>3 \times 1</m>. For <m>A\mathbf{y}</m> to be defined, the number of
            <em>columns</em> of <m>A</m> (here <m>2</m>) must match the number of components of
            <m>\mathbf{y}</m> (here <m>3</m>). They don't match, so the product is not defined.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <p>
    Alright, just one more page of matrices before we can finally get to some ODEs!
  </p>

